  {
   "id": "iKseA_4BAQAJ",
   "from": "mitchfirman",
   "author": "dba444e9364c1599",
   "date": "2020-03-01T19:07:57Z",
   "subject": "Re: [distance-sampling] Mark-recapture observer performance limits",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><br><br><div>Thanks Eric,</div><div><br></div><div>I had created a factored observer variable for the key function and was also using this as a covariate for the MR model...  This did produce different p0 values for the primary and secondary observers but it obviously didn&#39;t have the desired outcome as the primary was given a lower detection rate than the secondary (0.29 and and 0.35, respectively). As you suspected, when I dropped the MRmodel covariates, the p0s were identical. I switched to the actual &#39;observer&#39; variable for the MR model and all is well (p0 primary 0.80 and secondary 0.21).</div><div><br></div><div>I&#39;m assuming that using a factored observer variable (text) in the key function shouldn&#39;t skew those results?<br></div><div><br></div><div>Thanks again,</div><div>Mitch Firman<br></div><div><br></div><div>   <br></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "wHybHiNfAQAJ",
