     1,
     "\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div style=\"zoom:0%\">\r\n<div dir=\"auto\">yes, observer as a factor variable is the right approach<br>\r\n<br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div dir=\"auto\">Eric Rexstad<br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div dir=\"auto\">Centre for Research into Ecological and Environmental Modelling<br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div dir=\"auto\">University of St Andrews<br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div dir=\"auto\">St Andrews KY16 9LZ<br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div dir=\"auto\"><font size=\"2\">Registered charity:  SC013532</font></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div style=\"zoom:0%\">\r\n<div class=\"gmail_quote\">On Mar 1, 2020, at 7:08 PM, mitchfirman &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">mitchel...@gmail.com</a>&gt; wrote:\r\n<blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0pt 0pt 0pt 0.8ex;border-left:1px solid rgb(204,204,204);padding-left:1ex\">\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\"><br>\r\n<br>\r\n<div>Thanks Eric,</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>I had created a factored observer variable for the key function and was also using this as a covariate for the MR model...  This did produce different p0 values for the primary and secondary observers but it obviously didn&#39;t have the desired outcome as\r\n the primary was given a lower detection rate than the secondary (0.29 and and 0.35, respectively). As you suspected, when I dropped the MRmodel covariates, the p0s were identical. I switched to the actual &#39;observer&#39; variable for the MR model and all is well\r\n (p0 primary 0.80 and secondary 0.21).</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>I&#39;m assuming that using a factored observer variable (text) in the key function shouldn&#39;t skew those results?<br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>Thanks again,</div>\r\n<div>Mitch Firman<br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div>  <br>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n<p></p>\r\n</blockquote>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>"
